  "reasoning": "The claim that humans only use 10% of their brain capacity is a widely debunked neuroscientific myth. Modern neuroimaging techniques, such as functional Magnetic Resonance Imaging (fMRI) and Positron Emission Tomography (PET), demonstrate that virtually the entire brain is active throughout the day, even during simple tasks or sleep. Furthermore, evolutionary biology and clinical neurology show that the brain consumes approximately 20% of the body's energy despite accounting for only 2% of its mass; unused or dormant brain tissue naturally undergoes degeneration or atrophy. There is no large, dormant 90% region of the human brain.",
